and don't go get that one because you've got to cycle back around to the five.

00:34:21.740 --> 00:34:22.640
Does that make sense?

00:34:23.040 --> 00:34:31.140
Yeah. It sounds like an obvious thing to do, but it's very easy to get kind of caught up in the pressure when you're playing against an opponent

00:34:31.140 --> 00:34:35.640
where you're like, okay, I know I need that one, and it's the closest thing to me physically,

00:34:35.640 --> 00:34:38.800
so I'll cycle all the way back around to the one to get it.

00:34:38.800 --> 00:34:47.640
And you actually lose a lot of time by doing that rather than being as effective as possible by just hitting things as you cycle through.

00:34:47.760 --> 00:34:53.220
Because I don't know if we mentioned, but in order to switch die, you don't use the D-pad or something to go back and forth.

00:34:53.340 --> 00:34:59.180
You just hit the B button, I believe, and you advance one number.

00:34:59.280 --> 00:35:02.000
You go from one to two to two to three until you loop back around to one.

00:35:02.100 --> 00:35:02.960
You can't go backwards.

00:35:02.960 --> 00:35:08.880
Yeah, there should have been a dice peripheral that you could plug into the NES and you turn it to that side,

00:35:08.900 --> 00:35:11.020
and the game recognizes what side you're on.

00:35:11.340 --> 00:35:15.100
Perfect. So you want a habit?

00:35:16.180 --> 00:35:22.940
Yeah, I want you to give us one of the seven highly effective habits for Palamedes players.

00:35:24.720 --> 00:35:32.040
So basically, yeah, if you want to be a good Palamedes player, you have to make excellence your habit.

00:35:32.880 --> 00:35:38.060
I need you to work on your Palamedes game three, four hours a day.

00:35:38.320 --> 00:35:40.920
Then you got to have a balanced breakfast.

00:35:40.920 --> 00:35:43.700
You can't eat high fructose corn syrup.

00:35:44.660 --> 00:35:50.680
I want you to stop drinking, stop smoking.

00:35:51.050 --> 00:35:54.820
I need you to lock in on Palamedes.

00:35:55.180 --> 00:35:57.320
Make excellence your habit.

00:35:57.980 --> 00:36:00.600
Why can't Palamedes be at Evo next year?

00:36:02.960 --> 00:36:06.920
Center stage, 250 grand on the line, Palamedes.

00:36:07.220 --> 00:36:08.120
Let's do it.

00:36:09.140 --> 00:36:12.980
And then they announce at the event Palamedes 2.

00:36:13.220 --> 00:36:14.440
What would that entail?

00:36:14.700 --> 00:36:17.120
They don't have to do that, Sean, because it exists.

00:36:17.460 --> 00:36:20.940
It actually already came out on the Famicom.

00:36:20.940 --> 00:36:25.480
So technically, it could be a Bytes episode if we wanted it to be.

00:36:25.620 --> 00:36:39.100
But I'm going to explain the game because this is, despite how complicated we might make it sound, building hands by throwing dice and changing the dice to be the correct dice to match the dice on screen.

00:36:39.200 --> 00:36:40.340
It's not that complicated.

00:36:40.440 --> 00:36:41.300
It's a very straightforward game.

00:36:41.440 --> 00:36:43.160
What would a sequel be, right?

00:36:43.680 --> 00:36:45.880
Any guesses? Seven across.

00:36:46.300 --> 00:36:50.020
Is it that or it's not a D6?

00:36:50.020 --> 00:36:53.000
It's like a D8 or a D12 or something.

00:36:53.220 --> 00:36:55.340
It's the whole D&D gamut.

00:36:55.640 --> 00:37:08.100
Yeah. I appreciate both of those answers because I do think that they could have changed this game in a significant way other than what they decided to do for Palamedes 2 with the subtitle Star Twinkles.

00:37:08.300 --> 00:37:12.600
It's all about constellations in the sky and stuff like that.

00:37:13.200 --> 00:37:17.620
You go to the Big Dipper and the Bull and all that shit.

00:37:18.120 --> 00:37:19.740
The Bull. What is it?

00:37:20.320 --> 00:37:21.300
Taurus. Yeah.

00:37:21.500 --> 00:37:22.300
I was going to say that.

00:37:22.380 --> 00:37:23.280
I just didn't want to say that.

00:37:23.780 --> 00:37:25.480
Say it. I did say it.

00:37:25.800 --> 00:37:34.200
Technically, if the recording lines up, I should say it at exactly the same time as you so to prove that I know a little something about constellations of which I don't really know anything.

00:37:35.820 --> 00:37:36.820
Yeah. Go on.

00:37:36.820 --> 00:37:43.120
The big change in Palamedes 2 is that the dice no longer spawn from the top of the screen.

00:37:43.500 --> 00:37:46.320
That's right. The field rises from the bottom.

00:37:46.320 --> 00:37:55.260
You stand on that stack and it's like Star Wars A New Hope where the walls are closing in.

00:37:55.460 --> 00:38:05.380
You are now rising up to a different wall that's going to crush you as the bottom dice start to stack up and push you closer to the ceiling.

00:38:05.380 --> 00:38:10.080
You have to grab the dice from below you.

00:38:10.200 --> 00:38:12.560
So you can only grab the immediate line in front of you.

00:38:12.880 --> 00:38:20.560
Grab the dice from below you and throw them up into your slot tray, which is now up at the top to build your hand.

00:38:20.960 --> 00:38:24.800
But you now have only a hand of four instead of six.

00:38:24.800 --> 00:38:30.880
So they said, eh, let's just change it to make it smaller hands, which obviously makes it for much less permutations.

00:38:31.660 --> 00:38:35.880
But, and this is crazy, there is a new chain rule.

00:38:36.440 --> 00:38:45.480
You can only take a die equal to plus one or minus one from the last die taken from either player.

00:38:45.880 --> 00:38:47.380
What? Wait, say that again?

00:38:47.380 --> 00:38:51.920
I know it takes a long time to even get it in the game to get the feel.

00:38:52.140 --> 00:38:55.440
But let's say currently the game has just started.

00:38:55.620 --> 00:38:57.000
So nobody has taken a die.

00:38:57.320 --> 00:39:04.000
They will still put a die in the center of the top center of the screen of like what the round will start in.

00:39:04.100 --> 00:39:06.980
So let's say that is the number three.

00:39:07.600 --> 00:39:12.520
You can only play a three, a four or a two.

00:39:12.800 --> 00:39:14.420
That is all you can play.

00:39:14.420 --> 00:39:22.240
If you play something else, it gets rejected and it slowly falls back to the bottom of the screen, which is obviously very punishing to the player.

00:39:22.660 --> 00:39:30.980
But if your opponent plays four before you play two, the four gets registered, your two gets bumped.

00:39:31.180 --> 00:39:34.360
And now you either have to play a three, four or five.

00:39:34.500 --> 00:39:38.920
Oh, so the last die played is the North Star of like what?

00:39:38.920 --> 00:39:40.400
The North Star, you get it.

00:39:40.500 --> 00:39:41.960
The Star Twinkles.

00:39:42.320 --> 00:39:50.860
You have a constellation. It's actually insane that they change the game this much.

00:39:51.020 --> 00:39:52.000
I don't think it needed it.

00:39:52.420 --> 00:39:53.840
I really like that.

00:39:53.840 --> 00:39:55.500
I wish that was a game mode.

00:39:56.380 --> 00:40:01.540
You know what? I think it's like it's kind of like they made a game mode its own game.

00:40:02.220 --> 00:40:05.460
Like how, you know, like there's poker and there's Texas Hold'em.

00:40:05.580 --> 00:40:08.800
This is the Texas Hold'em to Palomitas' poker.

00:40:09.120 --> 00:40:10.020
Five cards done.

00:40:11.040 --> 00:40:18.360
But obviously with four dice to make up a hand, it's obviously like a lot faster too.

00:40:18.600 --> 00:40:23.440
The swings happen a lot quicker because you're building hands that are a lot smaller.

00:40:28.700 --> 00:40:33.640
It's not as, you know, like you could cash in earlier in the other game.

00:40:33.700 --> 00:40:36.080
You didn't have to purposely build something of six.

00:40:36.080 --> 00:40:40.300
But now with four, you can, both players are just playing faster.

00:40:40.300 --> 00:40:43.240
There's also a Game Boy version of Palomitas.

00:40:43.480 --> 00:40:54.560
It's the same core rules, but obviously because it's on the Game Boy, it's like much like tighter screen, which makes it feel faster, even though I don't think it's playing any faster.

00:40:56.520 --> 00:41:07.600
And then there's a bunch of like Japanese computer ports of this game, which means that it was obviously a little popular or interesting because there's also an arcade version came out around the same year.

00:41:07.840 --> 00:41:10.640
It might have been first, but it came out same year as the NES game.

00:41:10.780 --> 00:41:17.140
But those are all like the same rules, different presentation based on whatever computer they were on or arcade.

00:41:17.140 --> 00:41:30.780
So the Palomitas as a franchise is really just these two games with the first game seemingly being the more popular thing and maybe two changed up too much that like they couldn't justify a third one.

00:41:31.060 --> 00:41:37.860
Yeah, I'm stuck on the Game Boy version because I am just thinking about how much harder this game would be if it couldn't be color coded.

00:41:38.200 --> 00:41:41.960
Yeah, because it's just, I mean, you're just like, especially on a small Game Boy screen.

00:41:41.960 --> 00:41:51.520
I mean, it's probably like not, it's probably pretty easy to get a four confused with a five when you're trying to move that fast and you got, you know, it's just sides of the dice.

00:41:51.720 --> 00:42:05.960
I mean, I'm just thinking about turning this game into grayscale, which has probably, I don't know the difference in resolution between the NES and Game Boy, but it's probably illegible.

00:42:07.560 --> 00:42:11.880
Well, it's interesting because I think, I think it's actually fine.

00:42:12.020 --> 00:42:16.980
Like it's, it's pretty boring, colorless, which is funny that took Game Boy so long to have color.

00:42:17.320 --> 00:42:24.980
But they did do shading on the dice so that at least like not, they all don't have like six shades.

00:42:25.340 --> 00:42:30.480
No, no, that's what I'm saying. There's like, there's like two shades and it kind of cycles between odds and even numbers.

00:42:30.680 --> 00:42:41.040
Okay. But it still isn't like, it's not like it's more crunched in, you know, it's just like the amount of rows you have is probably less before you get crushed, if that makes sense.

00:42:41.200 --> 00:42:44.260
Like because they had to tighten it all, but still fit six across.

00:42:44.980 --> 00:42:48.900
Yeah. So I feel like there's only like five rows instead of nine.

00:42:49.260 --> 00:42:53.780
So silly. Let's see.

00:42:53.940 --> 00:42:55.800
Anything else? Anything else to say?

00:42:56.460 --> 00:42:57.400
I don't think so.

00:42:58.680 --> 00:43:03.100
Would you add like any house rules to your, to your multiplayer?

00:43:03.780 --> 00:43:06.700
Joe, should we have banned three pairs?

00:43:07.300 --> 00:43:11.440
No, I think that three pairs, pretty fair.

00:43:11.540 --> 00:43:14.360
And we were both using it and it was, it was working for both of us.

00:43:14.560 --> 00:43:22.540
I don't know. Yeah, you couldn't enforce it, but like, would there be a rule where it's like, you can't, you can't play repeat hands back to back.

00:43:22.680 --> 00:43:24.960
Like you can play three pair, but you have to play something in between.

00:43:24.960 --> 00:43:28.900
Yeah. I mean, as a house rule, that is hard.

00:43:28.920 --> 00:43:34.320
I could see that being like a, like a setting, you know, to, to make it more difficult.

00:43:34.640 --> 00:43:36.500
For the Evo 25 tournament mode.

00:43:36.960 --> 00:43:50.400
If, if somebody, if somebody said to you that one guy invented the alphabet, numbers, weights, measures, and coinage, would you take anything after that that they said seriously?

00:43:52.140 --> 00:43:52.960
Probably not.

00:43:53.140 --> 00:43:57.000
Why? Well, that's again, the manual for Palamedes.

00:43:57.300 --> 00:44:00.520
Oh, Palamedes also invented all that shit too on top of the die?

00:44:00.520 --> 00:44:04.160
Yeah, he invented all of that, um, including dice.

00:44:04.200 --> 00:44:08.000
But then they also say that primitive people would throw dice.

00:44:08.440 --> 00:44:12.180
So, uh, you know, take, take what, take from that what you will.

00:44:12.360 --> 00:44:16.100
I will not let, uh, this game's manual die.

00:44:16.800 --> 00:44:20.080
Do they still teach this game's manual in, in schools?

00:44:20.080 --> 00:44:21.580
Yeah. Yeah, actually.

00:44:21.940 --> 00:44:27.020
Um, at least in, uh, say it.

00:44:29.980 --> 00:44:35.260
Palamedes may have invented a lot of things, may not have invented dice, may not have even existed.

00:44:35.260 --> 00:44:40.880
But there is one thing that we invented that exists, and that is the essential games list.

00:44:44.200 --> 00:44:53.160
Look, I had a great time on the episode, um, which I mostly spent profusely apologizing to my best friend, Sean.

00:44:53.720 --> 00:44:56.420
So, hopefully he can find in his heart to forgive me.

00:44:56.660 --> 00:45:04.580
He talked a lot about the box and the cartridge and the manual, of which I guess I only perused a little bit of.

00:45:04.800 --> 00:45:09.580
But he never talked about the dice with realistic human legs and toes.

00:45:09.580 --> 00:45:11.640
I love how your essential vote is all about Sean.

00:45:12.080 --> 00:45:14.280
You're, like, talking about Sean and not the game.

00:45:14.320 --> 00:45:15.300
So, no, I'm just teeing up for him.

00:45:15.380 --> 00:45:16.640
I would love to hear. I know, I'm just kidding.

00:45:16.660 --> 00:45:20.300
I would love to hear as part of his vote, uh, his thoughts on these dice people.

00:45:20.660 --> 00:45:29.380
And if they, if maybe they could have been a franchise, maybe these could have been the mascots of whatever Hot B, the developer of this game, whatever console they would put out.

00:45:29.380 --> 00:45:32.660
Because that was all the rage in the 90s was just, put out your own console.

00:45:33.140 --> 00:45:34.400
Everybody's making them right now.

00:45:34.720 --> 00:45:36.860
3DO, Neo Geo.

00:45:37.480 --> 00:45:39.500
The Hot B. I mean, the Hot B was a console.

00:45:39.860 --> 00:45:40.740
The Hot E B.

00:45:40.920 --> 00:45:44.020
The Hot B. No, Hot B was the developer.

00:45:44.280 --> 00:45:45.820
And I think they made a pretty great game here.

00:45:46.200 --> 00:45:53.320
Um, or, or whatever, whatever that means in the, in the grand scheme of the essential games list.

00:45:53.320 --> 00:46:02.440
I don't think this game hits any, like, essentialness because it's just, like, it's a very replayable game for the people that enjoy it.

00:46:02.480 --> 00:46:11.260
And if you don't enjoy, basically, like, the, the action puzzle game they've laid down here, it's not really, like, a puzzle thing to solve.

00:46:11.400 --> 00:46:13.940
It's just kind of, like, a fast-thinking reflexes game.

00:46:14.100 --> 00:46:16.820
If you don't enjoy that, then there's nothing here.

00:46:16.940 --> 00:46:18.860
So, this is, like, an all-or-nothing thing.

00:46:19.080 --> 00:46:22.060
And so, I don't think I can put this on the essential games list.

00:46:22.060 --> 00:46:25.360
But it's, like, for me, this is, this is a great game.

00:46:25.480 --> 00:46:28.860
Like, this is, like, something I'm, like, happy I discovered and I will play.

00:46:29.560 --> 00:46:35.180
That's kind of similar to something I, like, might have said about Balloon Fight way back when.

00:46:35.280 --> 00:46:36.980
Of how it's just, like, it's so replayable.

00:46:37.080 --> 00:46:38.700
It's so pick-up and, and ready to go.

00:46:38.800 --> 00:46:51.380
But, like, that one is offering something that, like, mechanics-wise, like, can be enjoyed and can have, like, a longer stay than just the, the five-minute cycle of, like,

00:46:51.380 --> 00:46:52.700
well, I figured out what this game is.

00:46:52.800 --> 00:46:55.320
Like, you can't just, like, figure out what Balloon Fight is right away.

00:46:55.460 --> 00:46:58.760
This game, you can play, build a few hands, and figure out if it's for you or not.

00:46:59.640 --> 00:47:07.720
So, even though it's highly replayable from someone who enjoys it, I don't think it has, like, the kind of replayability that a game like Balloon Fight,

00:47:07.720 --> 00:47:14.860
which is going to be an essential game for as long as I can hold it on the list, it's not reaching that bar.

00:47:14.980 --> 00:47:16.520
So, I really enjoyed it.

00:47:16.660 --> 00:47:22.820
I look forward to hearing what everybody who played this game either growing up or because of this episode thought of it.

00:47:23.180 --> 00:47:24.400
So, please let us know.

00:47:24.560 --> 00:47:25.800
But it's a no from me.

00:47:25.940 --> 00:47:41.960
Sean? Yeah, I'll first note and acknowledge that the, like, dice people on the front of the manual, and I think on the front of the box of the game, are off-putting.

00:47:42.100 --> 00:47:46.300
They have very human-looking naked legs.

00:47:46.900 --> 00:47:48.820
They do wear gloves, though.

00:47:49.180 --> 00:47:50.480
Like, they don't wear socks.

00:47:50.500 --> 00:47:51.300
They don't wear shoes.

00:47:51.300 --> 00:47:56.520
They are, you see their toes, but they do wear white gloves, so it's okay.

00:47:57.580 --> 00:48:07.020
Um, anyway, for the game itself, I did find, like, you know, the core loop is, uh, it's satisfying.

00:48:07.820 --> 00:48:17.920
Uh, I think the game itself, though, and I was only playing against computer opponents, and I wasn't playing against, uh, my real human friends.

00:48:17.920 --> 00:48:22.920
Uh, but, I think that there's a pretty low skill ceiling.

00:48:23.220 --> 00:48:29.740
And, that means that I don't know how much enjoyment I would get out of this over time.

00:48:29.960 --> 00:48:34.840
Maybe if I did have human friends to play with, uh, I would have a different opinion.

00:48:35.360 --> 00:48:39.540
But, I do come to the same conclusion that it is not an essential game.

00:48:39.780 --> 00:48:43.100
Joe? I like this game more than I like Tetris.

00:48:43.580 --> 00:48:52.240
Um, I think that, again, having mostly experienced this in a two-player mode, when I think of something like Tetris, uh, Mike, I know you're pretty good at Tetris.

00:48:52.380 --> 00:48:53.940
I'm not very good at Tetris.

00:48:54.040 --> 00:48:57.200
If you and I played Tetris, you would destroy me.

00:48:58.580 --> 00:49:03.300
Partially because you're, you're so good at Tetris, and partially because I'm so bad at Tetris.

00:49:03.400 --> 00:49:06.760
You know what I mean? It's like, I'm playing against you, and I'm playing against the game.

00:49:07.300 --> 00:49:11.740
So, like, I think me playing against the game already hurts me if we're playing Tetris.

00:49:12.040 --> 00:49:21.620
Like, this game, that lower skill barrier means in a two-player setting, it's more, like, pure I'm playing against you.

00:49:21.860 --> 00:49:22.960
Does that make sense?

00:49:23.120 --> 00:49:30.620
Where it feels more like our skills are, it's like, we're going head-to-head, and, like, it's not as much about, like, who's good at the game.

00:49:30.700 --> 00:49:34.840
Because, like, anyone could be kind of good at the game, you know, if you're playing single-player in this.

00:49:34.840 --> 00:49:40.300
Um, I also just found, I don't love this, like, the Tetris style of game, personally.

00:49:40.480 --> 00:49:42.160
I just found this a little more interesting.

00:49:42.680 --> 00:49:49.840
But, yeah, overall, it's like, I enjoyed this game as I played it, but, like, it's, it's too, it's too simple for me.

00:49:49.900 --> 00:49:51.060
It doesn't hold my interest.

00:49:51.260 --> 00:49:52.560
These types of games never do.

00:49:52.920 --> 00:49:56.520
Um, if, if I didn't have to play it, I would never seek it out.

00:49:56.740 --> 00:50:02.160
Granted, I did surprisingly, like, I did surprisingly, like, want to go back to it a couple times after playing it,

00:50:02.200 --> 00:50:03.840
but that's already kind of worn off for me.

00:50:03.840 --> 00:50:05.760
So, I'm not going to vote it essential.

00:50:06.080 --> 00:50:09.460
Part of that, I think, is, uh, just my own, uh, preferences.

00:50:09.780 --> 00:50:12.560
But, I don't think I, I don't think I voted Tetris essential.

00:50:12.920 --> 00:50:19.120
Uh, so, even though I like a little more than Tetris, uh, still not up there for an essential game.

00:50:19.360 --> 00:50:24.400
I like your answer there about, you know, playing against the game and against the person.

00:50:24.400 --> 00:50:32.160
Because, you know, you're right, that, like, this game may just be simple enough that it's not, like, not as bare bones as, like, rock, paper, scissors, right?

00:50:32.220 --> 00:50:33.260
Like, anybody can play that.

00:50:33.260 --> 00:50:36.300
And so, we're just, I'm always playing against just you, not the game.

00:50:36.520 --> 00:50:43.300
Right? Like, there's a little more to it than that, but it's not as complicated as, they didn't figure out how to add T-spins yet to Palamedes.

00:50:43.300 --> 00:50:46.920
But, once they do, uh, the whole, the whole thing will be opened.

00:50:47.140 --> 00:50:48.380
Uh, you know, it's a good question, Joe.

00:50:48.480 --> 00:50:51.820
I don't know who voted, uh, for Tetris on the essential games list.

00:50:51.920 --> 00:50:53.180
I don't, I don't know if that happened.

00:50:53.280 --> 00:50:54.940
Did, did you? I wouldn't have.

00:50:55.340 --> 00:50:56.820
I think, uh, we didn't.

00:50:57.420 --> 00:50:58.940
No, no, it's not on there, right?

00:50:59.060 --> 00:51:02.340
I don't think it's on there. But, but I'm saying, like, did anybody even vote for it?

00:51:02.480 --> 00:51:03.420
Like, that's controversial.

00:51:03.900 --> 00:51:08.880
I think we all kind of just, uh, admitted that there were probably better ways to play Tetris.

00:51:09.080 --> 00:51:13.280
Right, now, now. I think, I do remember that, yeah, being, like, the NES version is not the best.

00:51:13.300 --> 00:51:15.780
But, I don't know if I would vote any version of Tetris essential.

00:51:16.060 --> 00:51:19.760
It's funny because, so, so, the NES version of Tetris, just to get into it for saying,

00:51:19.780 --> 00:51:22.780
because I'm sure our audience is still mad at us for not even considering it.

